Tear an Iceberg scan down without throwing.

The scan state's destructor waits for its manifest-reading tasks, and threw
what they threw. A destructor that throws ends the process (std::terminate).
At DuckDB's memory limit a task throws Out of Memory; the task executor keeps
that error, the scan's next call (TryGetNextBatch, FinishScanTasks) throws it
and the statement fails, and then the scan's teardown threw it again and took
every session of the process with it. The scan's calls surface the error; the
destructor now waits without throwing.

diff --git a/src/planning/iceberg_multi_file_list.cpp b/src/planning/iceberg_multi_file_list.cpp
index 7a9d57827..00b11e472 100644
--- a/src/planning/iceberg_multi_file_list.cpp
+++ b/src/planning/iceberg_multi_file_list.cpp
@@ -125,8 +125,17 @@ IcebergMultiFileListSharedState::IcebergMultiFileListSharedState(ClientContext &
 
 IcebergMultiFileListSharedState::~IcebergMultiFileListSharedState() {
 	if (data_manifest_read_state) {
-		//! FIXME: this could throw, if the tasks encountered an error
-		data_manifest_read_state->executor.WorkOnTasks();
+		//! Igloo: wait for the manifest-reading tasks, which use this state, without throwing what
+		//! they threw. A destructor that throws ends the process (std::terminate). At DuckDB's memory
+		//! limit a task throws Out of Memory; the executor keeps that error, and the scan's next call
+		//! (TryGetNextBatch, FinishScanTasks) throws it, failing the statement. Tearing the scan down
+		//! after that, or after the statement failed or was canceled anyway, waited here and threw
+		//! the kept error again, taking every session with it. The error belongs to the scan's calls,
+		//! which surface it; here there is nothing left to fail.
+		try {
+			data_manifest_read_state->executor.WorkOnTasks();
+		} catch (...) {
+		}
 	}
 }
 
